From: Surface Pro 11 audio bring-up
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: x1-microsoft-denali: Halve the DMIC clock

Mainline clocks the two far-field digital microphones at 4.8 MHz, and at
that rate capture carries a continuous broadband static that sits under
everything.  It is not a gain-staging artefact: it survives dropping the VA
decoder gain from +16 dB to unity, survives an 80 Hz to 8 kHz band-pass, and
is present in raw arecord output with no sound server running.  Halving the
clock to 2.4 MHz removes it and makes speech markedly clearer.

The property feeds only the VA macro's DMIC divider, so playback is
untouched.  Capture is still thin, which is a separate problem.

Taken from ooaklee/linux-surface-pro-11-oe,
patches/sp11-qcom-x1e-7.2-rc5-v2/0001-arm64-dts-qcom-x1-denali-use-2.4-MHz-DMIC-clock.patch,
where the two rates were compared on the machine.  Rebased onto 7.1.7, which
needed nothing beyond a line-number shift: the &lpass_vamacro node is
identical in both trees.

Nothing about this is Denali-specific in principle, so it may well be
superseded by a wider fix; check whether qcom,dmic-sample-rate still reads
4800000 upstream before carrying it forward.
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1-microsoft-denali.dtsi | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1-microsoft-denali.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1-microsoft-denali.dtsi
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1-microsoft-denali.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/x1-microsoft-denali.dtsi
@@ -867,7 +867,7 @@
 	pinctrl-names = "default";
 
 	vdd-micb-supply = <&vreg_l1b_1p8>;
-	qcom,dmic-sample-rate = <4800000>;
+	qcom,dmic-sample-rate = <2400000>;
 };
 
 &mdss {
